{"question": "Which countries have nuclear weapons, and how many?", "country": "", "voice_speed": 0.95, "gap": 0.4, "cover": ["9 COUNTRIES.", "12,187 NUKES.", "WHO HAS MOST?"], "region": "WORLD NUCLEAR FORCES, JAN 2026", "places": {"PYONGYANG": {"id": "PYONGYANG", "name": "Pyongyang", "lat": 39.03, "lon": 125.75}, "ISRAEL": {"id": "ISRAEL", "name": "Israel", "lat": 31.5, "lon": 34.9}, "ISLAMABAD": {"id": "ISLAMABAD", "name": "Islamabad", "lat": 33.69, "lon": 73.05}, "DELHI": {"id": "DELHI", "name": "New Delhi", "lat": 28.61, "lon": 77.21}, "LONDON": {"id": "LONDON", "name": "London", "lat": 51.51, "lon": -0.13}, "PARIS": {"id": "PARIS", "name": "Paris", "lat": 48.86, "lon": 2.35}, "BEIJING": {"id": "BEIJING", "name": "Beijing", "lat": 39.9, "lon": 116.4}}, "lines": [{"text": "Nine countries. 12,187 nuclear warheads. Here is who has them, from smallest to biggest.", "src": [0], "context": false, "note": "SIPRI Yearbook 2026", "scene": {"type": "wide"}}, {"text": "Number nine. North Korea. About 60, with material to build dozens more.", "src": [0], "context": false, "note": "SIPRI, Jan 2026", "scene": {"type": "pin", "place": "PYONGYANG", "head": "#9  North Korea", "notes": ["~60 warheads", "material for more"], "span": 13}}, {"text": "Eight. Israel. About 90, and it has never officially admitted to having any.", "src": [0], "context": true, "note": "SIPRI, Jan 2026", "scene": {"type": "pin", "place": "ISRAEL", "head": "#8  Israel", "notes": ["~90 warheads", "never confirmed"], "span": 13}}, {"text": "Seven. Pakistan. About 170.", "src": [0], "context": false, "note": "SIPRI, Jan 2026", "scene": {"type": "pin", "place": "ISLAMABAD", "head": "#7  Pakistan", "notes": ["~170 warheads"], "span": 13}}, {"text": "Six. India. About 190, just ahead of its neighbour.", "src": [0], "context": false, "note": "SIPRI, Jan 2026", "scene": {"type": "pin", "place": "DELHI", "head": "#6  India", "notes": ["~190 warheads"], "span": 13}}, {"text": "Five. The UK. 225, and every one of them goes to sea on submarines.", "src": [0], "context": true, "note": "SIPRI, Jan 2026", "scene": {"type": "pin", "place": "LONDON", "head": "#5  United Kingdom", "notes": ["225 warheads", "all at sea (Trident)"], "span": 13}}, {"text": "Four. France. About 370, the only nuclear power left in the European Union.", "src": [0], "context": true, "note": "SIPRI, Jan 2026", "scene": {"type": "pin", "place": "PARIS", "head": "#4  France", "notes": ["~370 warheads"], "span": 13}}, {"text": "Three. China. About 620, and growing faster than any other country.", "src": [0, 1], "context": false, "note": "SIPRI, Jan 2026", "scene": {"type": "pin", "place": "BEIJING", "head": "#3  China", "notes": ["~620 warheads", "fastest growing"], "span": 13}}, {"text": "Two. The United States. 5,042.", "src": [0], "context": false, "note": "SIPRI, Jan 2026", "scene": {"type": "number", "value": "5,042", "label": "#2  United States", "detail": "total warhead inventory", "flag": "US"}}, {"text": "And number one. Russia. 5,420.", "src": [0], "context": false, "note": "SIPRI, Jan 2026", "scene": {"type": "number", "value": "5,420", "label": "#1  Russia", "detail": "total warhead inventory", "flag": "RU"}}, {"text": "Between them, Russia and America hold 83 percent of the warheads ready for military use.", "src": [1], "context": false, "note": "SIPRI Yearbook 2026", "scene": {"type": "number", "value": "83%", "label": "held by Russia + USA", "detail": "of all military stockpiles", "flag": "RU"}}, {"text": "About 2,100 are kept on high alert, sitting on missiles.", "src": [1], "context": false, "note": "SIPRI Yearbook 2026", "scene": {"type": "number", "value": "2,100", "label": "on high operational alert", "detail": "mostly Russia and USA", "flag": "US"}}, {"text": "And the last treaty capping the big two ran out in February.", "src": [1], "context": false, "note": "SIPRI Yearbook 2026", "scene": {"type": "statement", "lines": ["New START: expired.", "February 2026.", "No cap left."], "flag": "RU"}}, {"text": "Nine countries. One planet. Which one worries you most?", "src": [], "context": true, "note": "", "scene": {"type": "wide"}}], "sources": [{"n": 0, "outlet": "SIPRI", "title": "SIPRI Yearbook 2026 summary: World nuclear forces, January 2026", "url": "https://www.sipri.org/sites/default/files/2026-06/yb26_summary_en_0.pdf"}, {"n": 1, "outlet": "SIPRI", "title": "Increasing focus on nuclear weapons amid heightened escalation risks: new SIPRI Yearbook out now", "url": "https://www.sipri.org/media/press-release/2026/increasing-focus-nuclear-weapons-amid-heightened-escalation-risks-new-sipri-yearbook-out-now"}], "caption": "Every nuclear-armed country, smallest to biggest. 9 countries, 12,187 warheads (SIPRI, Jan 2026). Russia and the US hold most of them, China is growing fastest, and the last US-Russia cap expired in February. Which one worries you most? Sources in the comments.", "hashtags": ["nuclear", "worldnews", "geopolitics", "military", "outpost"]}